{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1373821-how-to-tame-your-dragon","id":1373821,"title":"How to tame your Dragon","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-03-09T17:29:39-07:00","id":1373821,"title":"How to tame your Dragon","content":"My players went off on a hunt to kill a one-eyed Tyrant Lizard so that a Lunar could eat it's heart and acquire its power, that was planned. What wasn't planned was that they decided to capture one and sell it. Player Plans, amirite?<br /> <br /> Anyway, there aren't rules for taming animals anywhere I could find. One player has Friendship with Animals Approach which doesn't work on predators (saying that they merely let you move by unmolested), while Beast-Mastering Behavior lets to <b>train</b> a wild beast over the course of a week to follow your commands. but there's a gap in the actual catch-and-tame part. I let my player make a Survival roll to scare the beast into submission, then another to pacify it, then another to have it follow them as new-pack. I just pulled those out of thin air. <br /> <br /> How do you handle this?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Please be warned: this is not champagne, this is most likely a duck.<br /> -<i>Chausse</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#800080\"><i>Message me for Japanese translations.</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8830-sorcerous-overlord","name":"Sorcerous Overlord","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8830_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-09T18:10:18-07:00","id":1373825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sorcerous Overlord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1373821-how-to-tame-your-dragon#post1373821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, there aren't rules for taming animals anywhere I could find.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not entirely sure there's supposed to <i>be</i> an option to tame animals as such. The way I read the training rules (I use the ones in &quot;Latent and Magical Abilities&quot; in the Antagonists chapter, pp. 554-555, which are slightly different than the ones in the Survival charms section), animals just have an inherent &quot;wildness&quot; level, indicating how trainable they are as a species, and that's pretty much unchangeable. A tyrant lizard is just always going to be harder to work with than a wolf, and far harder than a dog or horse, something humans have actively bred to be docile and tractable. <br /> <br /> I think that having big scary animals like tyrant lizards also be harder to control and work with is probably a part of their balance. In fact, if an animal's owner took it into a notably different situation with lots of unusual stress and stimulation, I'd probably call for a roll to keep control of it - if you take your tyrant lizard into the marketplace of Nexus, you <i>should</i> have problems. That's just asking for a rampage. Being a familiar would be an exception to that, though I'd say that keeping an animal calm through the familiar bond would require concentration and focus, effectively a penalty on doing other stuff at the same time, and possibly risking an incident if you got into a fight or something else very distracting happened.<br /> <br /> All that said, I'd allow a couple of tricks to mitigate this. As &quot;generic&quot; latent abilities that most or all animals could learn, I'd let someone train a creature a) to be calm in a relatively specific situation (in combat, for example, or &quot;in a crowd of humans in an urban environment&quot;), and b) to be more docile in general. Training an animal for the first would mean that, in the specific situation trained, they wouldn't be stressed and freak out, and the owner wouldn't need to spend effort to keep them controlled. The second would lower the difficulty of keeping them calm and training them other tricks by 1 overall - they'd still possibly freak out in unusual situations, but it would be easier to keep them calm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-10T01:00:03-07:00","id":1373858,"title":"","content":"The rules for Social Influence are on page 213.<br /> <br /> A normal Tyrant Lizard has a Resolve of 3 and Guile 1.<br /> <br /> If I were running this as &quot;a thing&quot;, then absent Charms:<br /> <br /> 1. Appearance + Survival roll to Inspire an emotion of Respect or Calmness in the Tyrant Lizard. (Resolve 3 + 3 for a Major Intimacy of &quot;Nom&quot; + 2 Body Language Alone=) Difficulty 8. (Scaring them into submission is fine too, you sociopath! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />) [NB: If 8 is too harsh then use the Difficulty Chart on page 185 instead.]<br /> <br /> 2. Charisma + Survival roll to Instill a Tie of Loyalty to the character. Ideally you're presenting some food or other clear incentive for the Lizard to follow you, so Difficulty 3 (Resolve 3 - 2 for Inflamed Passion + 2 Body Language). I wouldn't allow bonus Appearance dice on this roll unless you look like a Tyrant Lizard. (Manipulation + Survival if it's fear based Loyalty.)<br /> <br /> You now have a Tyrant Lizard that won't attack you on sight.<br /> <br /> 3. Bargain or Pursaude roll (Charisma + Survival) to get the Lizard to do what you want.<br /> <br /> If you want to ride them, I say unlock that as a Latent ability: training animals in Latent abilities is page 554.<br /> <br /> There's some Deeb Survival Charms worth a look that could inspire custom Solar Charms.<br /> <br /> A Charm might render this down to a single roll. Appropriate stunts might change the dice pools/difficulties (want to Intimidate the Lizard with your vast Strength -- maybe roll Strength + Presence instead of Appearance + Survival). I don't feel that a Tyrant Lizard would spend Willpower in this situation (usually -- it may if its young are in danger or it's mating season or what-have-you). I can think of appropriate Resolve raising stunts, but as an ST I'd rather save them for narrating failed Social Influence rolls than for upping Difficulties. Exceptional Tyrant Lizards might have higher or lower stats and/or other applicable Intimacies.<br /> <br /> Use this as a baseline. Feel free to add or subtract from all the Difficulties to meet the needs of your game (Resolve 5 Lizard with Willpower and 3 point Stunt, up the &quot;Nom&quot; Intimacy to Defining for Difficulty 15 on the first roll for example, or swap the Defining Intimacy to &quot;Exalted are cool&quot; for Difficulty 2 on the first roll -- any value between 2 and 15 is 'canon' but you can homebrew higher or lower if required).<br /> <br /> If it's not narratively important, do it in a single roll (or hand-wave it). If it is centrally important do step one and two then switch to extended rolls over a period of weeks and months.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":531,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","user":"JohnDoe244","date":". 03-10-2020, 05:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-10T04:32:48-07:00","id":1373885,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1373821-how-to-tame-your-dragon?p=1373825#post1373825\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A tyrant lizard is just always going to be harder to work with than a wolf, and far harder than a dog or horse, something humans have actively bred to be docile and tractable.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but we tame all kinds of things. For a tyrant lizard, might be a good idea to take it as an egg and start working on it from the beginning.<br /> <br /> For an adult, I definitely think that food should be involved. Contrary to what Doctor Alan Grant said, T Rex doesn't want to hunt; no animal wants to hunt, it's exhausting and you're hungry most of the time. Make an animal think you're a reliable source of abundant food and it will calm down soon enough.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-10T08:25:23-07:00","id":1373934,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1373821-how-to-tame-your-dragon?p=1373858#post1373858\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The rules for Social Influence are on page 213.</div> </div> </div> </div>The social influence rules are an excellent point, and that is indeed how I'd do the initial taming. (What I suggested about the animal possibly freaking out even once it's tamed still stands, though.)<br /> <br /> Some other notes for social influence - I would actually say that all three social attributes could be used for most tasks, it just depends on how you stunt. Inspiring the initial emotion of calmness would be Appearance if you're just primarily trying to move slowly and peacefully, maintaining its interest without either spooking it or triggering its hunting instincts. Talking softly and peacefully, but also giving occasional firm commands (&quot;No!&quot; when it lunges forward suddenly) would be Charisma. And combining that with stuff that you know it's interested in, like presenting a corpse for it to eat and then working while it's sated and a bit sleepy, would be Manipulation.<br /> <br /> Rolling (Perception + Survival) to Read Intentions on the creature, to figure out what it already likes and dislikes, is a good idea. A tyrant lizard that hates and fears humans (probably had a few spears stuck in it at some point) is going to require a different sort of approach to one that's curious about humans, or one that just doesn't care much. Fortunately, the Guile penalty for not believing you're observed (-2) would apply to animals most of the time, I would think. Even when they know you're about, if they aren't in a confrontation with you, I don't think a typical animal is going to try to conceal its emotions.<br /> <br /> One Intimacy that I think a lot of animals probably have is at least a Minor one for creatures more powerful than themselves, with &quot;respect&quot;. That can be taken advantage of in the taming process, if you can put yourself in that category. Of course, with a tyrant lizard, that's tricky. You may have to fight it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-10T08:45:20-07:00","id":1373937,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1373821-how-to-tame-your-dragon?p=1373934#post1373934\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One Intimacy that I think a lot of animals probably have is at least a Minor one for creatures more powerful than themselves, with &amp;quot;respect&amp;quot;. </div> </div> </div> </div>Many animals seem to have more sophisticated behaviour than is often attributed to them, but I think respect is a bit too abstract a concept for them, and what does &quot;more powerful&quot; even necessarily mean?<br /> <br /> As tyrant lizards go, I think they're still going to form relationships primarily from the template of a solitary predator, they're not going to have a strong inherent basis for hierarchical social structures. <br /> <br /> Barring cases where the skills of the Exalted are employed, taming a lot of animals does kind of come down to conditioning them through sustained abuse, and that sounds very much like what would happen if one is continuously fighting a dinosaur for dominance. That's not going to be &quot;this person is my Bro&quot; it's &quot;if I do doing the wrong thing it'll hurt less&quot;.<br /> <br /> Not necessarily a thing the Exalted need to do though, they've got means to kind of appeal more directly to whatever an animal's emotional core is our communicate on its level.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}